      "title": "Wasson Finds JLC Tutoring Rewarding",
      "description": "Wasson Finds JLC Tutoring Rewarding  - University of the Ozarks Skip to main content Enable accessibility for low vision Open the accessibility menu Skip to main content Join us for Homecoming! October 15-18 Click here to check out the details! Ozarks Experience Ozarks Email Calendar Directory TSC Search Ozarks.edu: Narrow your search by content type Page Degrees Events News Offices & Services Faculty & Staff Links Menu University of the Ozarks Home Admissions & Aid Show sub-menu Learn about our admissions process, financial aid and begin your Ozarks journey. Admissions & Aid Costs & Aid How to Apply Visit Campus New Student Orientation & Move-In High School Juniors Incoming Freshman Transfer Students International Students Admitted Students Calendar Apply Academics Show sub-menu Learn about our unique academic experience and the academic opportunities here at University of the Ozarks. Academics Our Academic Experience Degrees & Programs LENS Program Compass Study Abroad Library Canvas Student Success Center Jones Learning Center Faculty Directory Calendar Apply Life at Ozarks Show sub-menu Explore what makes life at Ozarks so special. Life at Ozarks Important Dates New Student Orientation & Move-In Student Engagement Residential Life & Housing Dining Shuttle Services Campus Recreation Spiritual Life Career Services Counseling Services Campus & Region Map Public Safety Title IX Calendar Apply Athletics Show sub-menu Our student-athletes strive for excellence on and off of the playing field, court or pool. Athletics Athletics Website Intercollegiate Sports Schedules Athlete Recruitment Eagle Tuesdays Fitness Facilities Support Athletics Recruit Me Support Show sub-menu Your gifts to University of the Ozarks truly matter to our students! Support Ways to Support Green Giving Update your info Grants and Initiatives Advancement Staff Hire an Eagle 1834 Society Planned Giving Give Bookstore Show sub-menu Our campus store located in Robson Library next to Campus Perks. Shop Now Conference Services Show sub-menu Ozarks offers a prime location for a variety of events. Contact us today to see how we can help make your next event a success. Conference Services Conferences and Meetings Weddings and Receptions Camps Catering Internal Events Contact Us! About Show sub-menu Learn about our thriving university, staff and history. About Leadership History Mission and Vision Our Christian Heritage Board of Trustees Rankings and Accreditations Student Outcomes Strategic Plan News Location & Directions Personnel Directory Offices & Services Marketing and Public Relations Reporting Calendar Request Info Search Ozarks.edu: Links Current Students Admitted Students Parents Faculty and Staff Alumni Search Request Info Visit Give Apply Home / News / Wasson Finds JLC Tutoring Rewarding Wasson Finds JLC Tutoring Rewarding 3 days ago • September 11, 2026 By Larry Isch Posted in JLC For University of the Ozarks junior Madi Wasson, tutoring fellow students through the Jones Learning Center has become more than a way to help others succeed. Over the past two years, the experience has also strengthened her own academic skills and given her valuable preparation for a future career in the classroom. An English major from St. Paul, Ark., Wasson plans to become a high school English teacher and theatre director. With minors in education and psychology, she said working one-on-one with students has given her an early glimpse into the rewards and challenges of teaching. “Being a tutor has not only been very rewarding, but I’ve been really surprised how much it has helped me in my own studies,” she said. “When you’re trying to explain something to someone, you have to make sure you know the subject as well as possible, and that’s helped me in my classes. I’ve also learned patience and how to better explain things to others. Those are the type of lessons that will serve me well as a future teacher.” Wasson began working in the JLC writing lab as a sophomore and has tutored students in subjects ranging from literature and academic writing to theatre-related courses. Along the way, she has discovered that helping others work through academic challenges has reinforced many of the same skills she will need as an educator. “Tutoring has definitely helped me come out of my shell more and to be more open to meeting and talking to people,” Wasson said. “Working with students from different backgrounds and with various learning differences has also helped me become more understanding and more patient to those who have learning challenges. I think it’s helped me become a better teacher and a better person.” One area Wasson particularly enjoys is helping students navigate the research and writing process, including finding credible sources, references and citations for academic papers. She also helps students become more comfortable with the various writing and citation styles required in their courses. “I like to tell them that they don’t have to rely on AI and that there’s all sort of resources and tools out there to help them,” she said. “I also work with a lot of students on different writing styles, such as APA, MLA or Chicago styles, depending on the class they’re in. It’s always nice to see the relief on their faces when they realize that it’s not as difficult as they thought and that they can do it.” During her tutoring sessions, Wasson has found herself inadvertently in the role of counselor and mentor to freshmen students trying to navigate the early weeks of college life. “I was working with one freshman girl and she was really struggling; she wasn’t sure she wanted to stay in school,” Wasson said. “I just tried to explain to her that the first few weeks are always tough, and that we’ve all been in her shoes. I told her that I felt the exact same way when I was a freshman and that it gets better as you meet more people and start building relationships on campus. I think she appreciated hearing those things.” Wasson, whose younger sister, Kate, is a freshman at Ozarks,  said working with JLC students has made her college experience even more rewarding. “To know that I’m playing even a small role in a student’s success in college makes me feel really good,” she said. “The JLC is a great program and it is helping students who might not have had an opportunity of going to college actually get a college degree. I’m proud to be a part of that program.” Topics: JLC Share this story News Categories News Categories Select News Category About  (866) Academic Support  (34) Academics  (292) Accounting  (9) Admission  (32) Alumni  (218) Alumni Stories  (42) American Studies  (4) Art  (168) Athletics  (2,827) Biology  (73) Business Administration  (92) Business Education  (1) Campaign  (17) Campus Life  (39) Career Services  (17) Chapel  (46) Chemistry  (26) Communication Studies  (84) Community Events  (404) Community Service  (66) Computer Science  (6) Cost & Aid  (3) COVID-19  (8) Creative Writing / Thought  (6) Criminal Justice  (4) Donor Spotlights  (8) Economics  (3) Education  (73) Elementary Education  (12) English  (22) Environmental Studies  (39) Exercise Science  (1) Exhibit  (70) Faculty Spotlights  (51) Film Studies  (4) Finance  (1) Financial Aid  (15) Giving  (99) Graphic Design  (1) Greystone  (3) Health Science  (35) History  (13) Information Science  (2) Interfaith Studies  (14) International  (16) Internships  (21) JLC  (54) Management  (9) Marketing  (15) Mathematics  (35) Media Production  (8) Military Science  (3) Music  (52) Music Performance  (15) Music Studies  (4) Outdoors  (25) Parents  (9) PE  (4) Philosophy  (15) Photography  (5) Physical Education  (4) Physics  (7) Political Science  (48) Presbyterian  (47) Psychology  (40) Religion  (17) Robson Library  (2) ROTC  (2) Scholarship  (32) Skills Academy  (1) Sociology  (14) Spanish  (16) Strategic Communication  (3) Student Events  (126) Student Life  (35) Student Organizations  (100) Student Stories  (6