    "https://ozarks.edu/news/author-illustrator-to-discuss-new-book-on-arkansas-women-musicians/": {
      "title": "Author, Illustrator to Discuss New Book on Arkansas Women Musicians",
      "description": "Author, Illustrator to Discuss New Book on Arkansas Women Musicians - University of the Ozarks Skip to main content Enable accessibility for low vision Open the accessibility menu Skip to main content Join us for Homecoming! October 15-18 Click here to check out the details! Ozarks Experience Ozarks Email Calendar Directory TSC Search Ozarks.edu: Narrow your search by content type Page Degrees Events News Offices & Services Faculty & Staff Links Menu University of the Ozarks Home Admissions & Aid Show sub-menu Learn about our admissions process, financial aid and begin your Ozarks journey. Admissions & Aid Costs & Aid How to Apply Visit Campus New Student Orientation & Move-In High School Juniors Incoming Freshman Transfer Students International Students Admitted Students Calendar Apply Academics Show sub-menu Learn about our unique academic experience and the academic opportunities here at University of the Ozarks. Academics Our Academic Experience Degrees & Programs LENS Program Compass Study Abroad Library Canvas Student Success Center Jones Learning Center Faculty Directory Calendar Apply Life at Ozarks Show sub-menu Explore what makes life at Ozarks so special. Life at Ozarks Important Dates New Student Orientation & Move-In Student Engagement Residential Life & Housing Dining Shuttle Services Campus Recreation Spiritual Life Career Services Counseling Services Campus & Region Map Public Safety Title IX Calendar Apply Athletics Show sub-menu Our student-athletes strive for excellence on and off of the playing field, court or pool. Athletics Athletics Website Intercollegiate Sports Schedules Athlete Recruitment Eagle Tuesdays Fitness Facilities Support Athletics Recruit Me Support Show sub-menu Your gifts to University of the Ozarks truly matter to our students! Support Ways to Support Green Giving Update your info Grants and Initiatives Advancement Staff Hire an Eagle 1834 Society Planned Giving Give Bookstore Show sub-menu Our campus store located in Robson Library next to Campus Perks. Shop Now Conference Services Show sub-menu Ozarks offers a prime location for a variety of events. Contact us today to see how we can help make your next event a success. Conference Services Conferences and Meetings Weddings and Receptions Camps Catering Internal Events Contact Us! About Show sub-menu Learn about our thriving university, staff and history. About Leadership History Mission and Vision Our Christian Heritage Board of Trustees Rankings and Accreditations Student Outcomes Strategic Plan News Location & Directions Personnel Directory Offices & Services Marketing and Public Relations Reporting Calendar Request Info Search Ozarks.edu: Links Current Students Admitted Students Parents Faculty and Staff Alumni Search Request Info Visit Give Apply Home / News / Author, Illustrator to Discuss New Book on Arkansas Women Musicians Author, Illustrator to Discuss New Book on Arkansas Women Musicians 16 minutes ago • September 16, 2026 By Larry Isch Author Stephen Koch and illustrator Katherine Strause will discuss their new book, “ From Almeda To Zilphia: Arkansas Women Who Transformed American Popular Song ,” at University of the Ozarks on Wednesday, Oct. 7, as part of the University’s 2026-27 Walton Arts & Ideas Series. The event will begin at 7 p.m. in the Rogers Conference Center. There is no cost for admission and the public is invited to attend. There will be books available for purchase. The book profiles artists who are connected to Arkansas and who have influenced music around the world in ways still being discovered. It has received both the Association for Recorded Sound Collections Award for Excellence for Best Research in Recorded Popular Music and the Arkansas State Library’s Arkansiana Award. Despite their collective cultural impact, these women’s stories have too often been overlooked, forgotten, or never told, according to Koch. Part history, part art, and part music, the book features a wide cross-section of inspirational creatives across the decades. With surprises like Maya Angelou’s side career as a calypso singer and Carrie Cash, who recognized that her son, Johnny, had “the gift,” “From Almeda To Zilphia” covers a wide variety of musical styles, including Gospel-Rosetta Tharpe, Opera-Marjorie Lawrence, Blues-Sippie Wallace, Folk Ballads-Almeda Riddle, Dance-Pop-Beth Ditto, Classical-Florence Price, and Country-K.T. Oslin. One of the women featured in the book is Zilphia (Johnson) Horton, a 1931 music graduate of U of O (then The College of the Ozarks) who went on to become an influential educator, folklorist, musician and social justice activist in Tennessee in the 1940s and 50s. The Paris, Ark.-native promoted the use of folksongs and hymns in the labor and civil rights movements, notably, “We Shall Overcome,” and “We Shall Not Be Moved.” Working from often hard-to-find historic photographs, vivid portraits by renowned artist Strause of the women profiled make plain the strength and artistry of these cultural pioneers. With a thoughtful forward by professor Dr. Cherisse Jones-Branch and a heart-wrenching afterword by acclaimed country music songwriter Erin Enderlin, “ From Almeda To Zilphia ” is written by musician and award-winning journalist Koch, writer and host of the syndicated public radio program Arkansongs. Koch has been involved in recognition and preservation efforts of forgotten musical greats, especially Louis Jordan, the ground-breaking jazz/R&B pioneer, authoring the biography “ Louis Jordan: Son of Arkansas, Father of R&B, ” a Jordan musical, “Jump!,” a Jordan documentary film, “Is You Is,” and helping create a Louis Jordan Memorial Highway near Jordan’s native Brinkley, which is also home to a bust of Jordan, funded by Koch’s annual Louis Jordan Tribute concerts. He consults with communities to help them promote their cultural legacies. He wrote and narrated the documentary film “Music In Arkansas Origins: 200 B.C.-1941 A.D.,” airing on PBS. Strause is a painter based in Little Rock, Ark., whose work explores themes of memory, resilience, identity, and the human presence through richly layered oil paintings. Her figurative work examines the emotional and psychological landscape of contemporary life, drawing on personal experience, history, and the expressive possibilities of paint. Working exclusively in oil, she builds luminous surfaces that balance observation with abstraction, inviting viewers to consider the quiet strength and complexity of her subjects. Strause has exhibited her work throughout the South and Midwest in museums, universities, and galleries. Share this story News Categories News Categories Select News Category About  (866) Academic Support  (34) Academics  (292) Accounting  (9) Admission  (32) Alumni  (219) Alumni Stories  (42) American Studies  (4) Art  (168) Athletics  (2,827) Biology  (73) Business Administration  (92) Business Education  (1) Campaign  (17) Campus Life  (39) Career Services  (17) Chapel  (46) Chemistry  (26) Communication Studies  (84) Community Events  (404) Community Service  (66) Computer Science  (6) Cost & Aid  (3) COVID-19  (8) Creative Writing / Thought  (6) Criminal Justice  (4) Donor Spotlights  (8) Economics  (3) Education  (73) Elementary Education  (12) English  (22) Environmental Studies  (39) Exercise Science  (1) Exhibit  (70) Faculty Spotlights  (51) Film Studies  (4) Finance  (1) Financial Aid  (15) Giving  (99) Graphic Design  (1) Greystone  (3) Health Science  (35) History  (13) Information Science  (2) Interfaith Studies  (14) International  (16) Internships  (21) JLC  (54) Management  (9) Marketing  (15) Mathematics  (35) Media Production  (8) Military Science  (3) Music  (52) Music Performance  (15) Music Studies  (4) Outdoors  (25) Parents  (9) PE  (4) Philosophy  (15) Photography  (5) Physical Education  (4) Physics  (7) Political Science  (48) Presbyterian  (47) Psychology  (40) Religion  (17) Robson Library  (2) ROTC  (2) Scholarship 